<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dietrich Klagges</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1891">1891</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dietrich Klagges was a German Nazi politician who served as interior minister of the Free State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> from 1931 and as its minister-president from 1933 to 1945. In February 1932 he appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> to a post in Brunswick's legation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, giving the stateless Hitler German citizenship so that he could run for president. After the war he was convicted for crimes committed under his rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dietrich-klagges.jpg" alt="Dietrich Klagges" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Artmann, Braunschweig · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dietrich_Klagges.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
